WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.543 --> 00:00:04.254
- This extraordinary year

00:00:04.296 --> 00:00:07.132
has shown us that wherever we step back,

00:00:07.174 --> 00:00:09.051
the space we leave

00:00:09.092 --> 00:00:12.763
is providing wildlife
with new opportunities.

00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:18.810
[sweeping orchestral music]

00:00:20.103 --> 00:00:22.439
- This pandemic can help us
take a step back

00:00:22.481 --> 00:00:24.566
and assess the relationship

00:00:24.608 --> 00:00:26.944
that humans want to have
with the natural world.

00:00:28.403 --> 00:00:31.073
Juneau is a premier
whale watching destination.

00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:35.786
In a typical year, we'll have
about 1.3 million people

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:37.037
visit by cruise ship.

00:00:37.079 --> 00:00:40.499
And of those, a quarter of them
will board smaller boats

00:00:40.541 --> 00:00:42.501
to go on whale watching trips.

00:00:42.543 --> 00:00:44.711
This year, we essentially have zero.

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:48.131
This year is
a really incredible opportunity

00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:49.007
as a scientist

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:53.178
to collect data in the absence
of a lot of boats on the water,

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.390
that we can then compare
to previous and future years

00:00:56.431 --> 00:00:58.433
of normal whale watching activity.

00:00:58.475 --> 00:01:01.728
- The number of whale watching boats
around a whale

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.982
increases the amount of sound
that a whale is exposed to.

00:01:05.023 --> 00:01:08.402
Therefore, there's the potential
for whales to be more stressed out

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:11.113
than they would if they had
less disturbance around them.

00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:14.908
- The goal of the study is to determine
what is the stress hormone level

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.536
of these whales
during this unusual year.

00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:20.706
- What we're looking for

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:24.293
is the concentration
of a steroid hormone called cortisol,

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:26.795
and we recognize this as stress.

00:01:26.837 --> 00:01:28.881
Power two, safety off.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.591
Let's try and get a little bit closer.

00:01:30.632 --> 00:01:32.301
- When we're collecting our data,

00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:35.304
we believe we have
a fairly minimal impact on the whale,

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:37.514
so the biopsy samples
that we take--

00:01:37.556 --> 00:01:39.516
I would compare that
to getting a finger prick

00:01:39.558 --> 00:01:40.392
at the doctor's office,

00:01:40.434 --> 00:01:41.768
so it hurts
for just a split second,

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.062
but then, after that, you're fine,
and you forget about it.

00:01:46.273 --> 00:01:48.442
- We fire this towards the animal.

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:51.486
It hits into the side of the animal,
and then it bounces off

00:01:51.528 --> 00:01:53.614
and floats in the water.

00:01:53.655 --> 00:01:55.157
- It's leaking blubber.

00:01:55.199 --> 00:01:57.451
- And when we get it, we have
skin and blubber

00:01:57.492 --> 00:01:58.702
in this coring end.

00:01:58.744 --> 00:02:00.662
Okay, so I'll just hold on to it.
You twist.

00:02:00.704 --> 00:02:02.664
It's a pretty small sample.
It's a little smaller

00:02:02.706 --> 00:02:04.082
than the eraser end of a pencil.

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.250
Think we got it all.
Nice job.

00:02:05.292 --> 00:02:06.335
- All right, good sample.

00:02:08.086 --> 00:02:12.174
Our results might help to inform
management, regulations, and guidelines

00:02:12.216 --> 00:02:13.509
for humpback whales in Juneau,

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:17.262
so perhaps changes
to the number of boats around whales,

00:02:17.304 --> 00:02:19.431
how long boats stay with whales,

00:02:19.473 --> 00:02:22.184
and overall, how many boats
are on the water.

00:02:23.727 --> 00:02:26.522
This moment provides
a really unique opportunity

00:02:26.563 --> 00:02:28.649
to understand the natural state

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.235
of whales and other wildlife
around the globe.

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:34.238
- We have an opportunity
to slow down a little bit

00:02:34.279 --> 00:02:37.491
and think about how we interact
with wildlife

00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:40.452
and what that might look like
with a little bit more intention.

00:02:40.494 --> 00:02:46.500
[gentle music]

